//? Title: RepVGG: Tensor Branch Fusion and Deployment After Explicit Fitting
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
RepVGG trains with several aligned convolution/BN branches and deploys as a plain 3×3 stack.
We verify Float64 fusion and every source derivative, check complete model counts, then
explicitly fit A0 and fuse the validation-selected trained checkpoint before test comparison.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.5_attention_and_modern_convnets/repvgg.cpp`.

> The method

A training-form block sums a bias-free 3×3/BN, a bias-free 1×1/BN and, when widths and stride
permit, an identity/BN branch, then applies ReLU. Symmetric pad one aligns the 3×3 centres
with the unpadded 1×1 at stride one or two. All BN layers learn scale/shift with epsilon 1e-5.
At inference, moments are fixed, so each branch folds into a biased convolution:

$$s=\gamma/\sqrt{v+10^{-5}},\qquad W'=Ws,\qquad b'=\beta-\mu s.$$
\alttext(Fixed batch-normalization moments and affine values scale the filter and supply its bias.)

Pad the 1×1 filter to the 3×3 centre, turn identity into a grouped one-hot centre kernel,
fold its BN, then SUM all kernels and biases. ReLU stays outside. This conversion preserves
INFERENCE behavior with saved moments; it does not replace batch-dependent training BN.
The implementation uses tensor folding, padding, one-hot kernels and addition, preserving
Float32/Float64 without host weight arrays. Functional folding also preserves derivatives
with respect to the original filters, gamma/beta and input, as checked in four Float64 fixtures.
DeployRepVGG separately detaches independent deployment leaves and copies classifier weights.

A0 has a stem and stages repeating 2/4/14/1, for 22 blocks total, widths 48/96/192/1280
and a 48-channel stem. Every stage begins with stride two; the reference stem also uses two.
A1/A2 scale widths, while B variants repeat 4/6/16/1. g2/g4 variants group only even block
indices 2..26 after the stem. He-uniform initialization and the biased global-mean classifier
follow the source. Full A0 has 9108968 learned plus 23648 BN-state scalars; deployment has
8309384 learned and no BN state. All ten actual full-weight training and deployment counts
are checked at 32 pixels, releasing one form before allocating the other. Reference 224-pixel
convolution output sides are exactly seven times these maps, so MACs scale by 49 while
classifier work stays fixed. A0 also executes at 224 pixels. Larger full-resolution forwards
and latency measurements are not claimed; MACs omit BN/activation/addition/memory work.

The full CIFAR A0 fit changes stem stride to one and classes to ten, retaining all 22 blocks
and four stage downsamplings, with 7840778 training parameters. Its visible loop uses 64 fit,
64 validation and 64 official-test images, six batch-eight epochs (48 updates, kept short so the
report runs in seconds), tensor Adam(.001), explicit
kernel L2=.0001/2 and independent padded crops/flips. Validation selects paired learned/BN
checkpoints. Only THEN does visible code fuse that trained checkpoint into 7041194 deployed
scalars and compare all 64 test logits/predictions with training-form inference. This verifies
actual fitted conversion, without claiming the small subset reproduces paper accuracy or speed.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/repvgg_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/repvgg_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/repvgg_concept.tikz > build/repvgg_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/repvgg_concept.svg build/repvgg_concept.dvi > build/repvgg_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
